[[:db/retract 17592186172390 :question/answer-fields 17592186169208] [:db/retract 17592186172390 :question/answer-fields 17592186169218] [:db/retract 17592186172390 :question/answer-fields 17592186169221] [:db/retract 17592186172390 :question/answer-fields 17592186169214] {:db/id 17592186171919 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4c2743b2-72ab-5576-b421-aa5dcedd6c11" "question-1261accc-9253-5b2b-9450-d1cf9dd3a558" "question-003c5be3-9b52-54de-b5e9-0ebd26752121" "question-31706254-2d24-50b4-a836-dd024a83aac7" "question-d1a3ce27-2b7c-51c0-8626-2bd58e3fc009"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171718 :question/problem "Do the sets below have the same cardinality, and why?\n\n$$\nA=\\{ 4n \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 2, 4,  \\ldots, 20 \\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinalities of two sets cannot be equal if one set is finite and the other is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, notice that \n\n- $A=\\{ 0, \\pm 4, \\pm 8, \\ldots \\}$ is infinite, while\n\n- $B$ is finite with  $|B| = 10.$\n\nTherefore, $|A| \\neq |B|.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nSince $A$ is infinite and $B$ is finite, we have that $|A| \\neq |B|$." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171920 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-99114540-20ff-5212-8b42-4abfffd44d45" "question-e32b700f-379c-5d3a-b0db-4859531b5dcb" "question-2a225633-5b65-55d6-85ca-1c31e57a48fb" "question-b75659ad-fe25-5e83-878e-b9945eaf6be7" "question-1e8f5aa7-7ccd-5ed3-b543-c8ead40f433d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171719 :question/problem "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$$\nA= \\{ -7, -2, 3, 8, \\ldots  \\}, \\qquad B = \\mathbb{N}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5 \\\\  & =-2-(-7) \\\\  & =3-(-2) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-7+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-7+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{5n-12 \\mid \\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $8$ | $\\cdots$ | $5n-12$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in B,$ we have $f(n) = 5n-12 \\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171921 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ec2d5fbd-6cc2-569f-94cc-7ef69e7f43a0" "question-7c2c4f15-f70f-5bf2-aa53-6e682a54a3eb" "question-a1686501-a304-5a7a-bf6f-4c7790651929" "question-f930f3c4-3dbb-5481-a48e-987b268b6aa0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171744 :question/problem "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$$\nA = \\mathbb{N}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{1,\\, \\dfrac12,\\,  \\dfrac14,\\,  \\dfrac18,\\,  \\ldots \\right \\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{  \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\,  \\Bigg |\\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1}$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in A,$ we have $f(n) =   \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c2743b2-72ab-5576-b421-aa5dcedd6c11" :question/id #uuid "4c2743b2-72ab-5576-b421-aa5dcedd6c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-181626" :question/problem "$$\nS={1,1,2,3,{4,5}},\\,T={n\\in N∣\\,2\\le n\\le 7}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $S$ and $T$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $|S|=4$\n- $|T|=5$\n- $|S|=|T|$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,  $S$ is a finite set and $|S|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $T={2,3,4,5,6,7}$ is a finite set and $|T|=6.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $4\\ne 6,$ we conclude that $|S|\\ne |T|.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-366327fe-3c8d-5196-b31e-017b57838927"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-366327fe-3c8d-5196-b31e-017b57838927" :answer-field/id #uuid "366327fe-3c8d-5196-b31e-017b57838927" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16800456-6759-58c6-a585-7041bbe5d62e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d96d5e4e-6751-5dec-b3c1-3719c09df64e" "answer-16800456-6759-58c6-a585-7041bbe5d62e" "answer-a15fa94e-2fd4-55b5-8a43-b2bdf463ba84" "answer-b6a3cd4d-640f-57c2-a20a-46482aeaf94b" "answer-f0b46f19-d690-587d-9747-1e56dd29c668"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d96d5e4e-6751-5dec-b3c1-3719c09df64e" :answer/id #uuid "d96d5e4e-6751-5dec-b3c1-3719c09df64e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16800456-6759-58c6-a585-7041bbe5d62e" :answer/id #uuid "16800456-6759-58c6-a585-7041bbe5d62e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a15fa94e-2fd4-55b5-8a43-b2bdf463ba84" :answer/id #uuid "a15fa94e-2fd4-55b5-8a43-b2bdf463ba84" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6a3cd4d-640f-57c2-a20a-46482aeaf94b" :answer/id #uuid "b6a3cd4d-640f-57c2-a20a-46482aeaf94b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0b46f19-d690-587d-9747-1e56dd29c668" :answer/id #uuid "f0b46f19-d690-587d-9747-1e56dd29c668" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1261accc-9253-5b2b-9450-d1cf9dd3a558" :question/id #uuid "1261accc-9253-5b2b-9450-d1cf9dd3a558" :question/math-academy-id "q-181624" :question/problem "$$\nX={a,b,2,4},\\,Y={n\\in N∣\\,1\\le n<5}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $X$ and $Y$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $|X|=4$\n- $|Y|=5$\n- $|X|=|Y|$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X$ is a finite set and $|X|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $Y={1,2,3,4}$ is a finite set and $|Y|=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $X$ and $Y$ are finite sets and  $|X|=|Y|=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b0ec5345-dc55-53da-93f5-32f3809241ac"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b0ec5345-dc55-53da-93f5-32f3809241ac" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ec5345-dc55-53da-93f5-32f3809241ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5cf6f434-9fed-5970-b8e2-d17962421f47" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5cf6f434-9fed-5970-b8e2-d17962421f47" "answer-6e02cd42-42d0-5705-a005-75524f936d99" "answer-afae2ee4-4ec6-5251-afbc-9e17da7a7304" "answer-ab558918-6e8c-59eb-9a7e-4397683388cc" "answer-411917dc-6c93-55d0-978d-0767736c6121"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cf6f434-9fed-5970-b8e2-d17962421f47" :answer/id #uuid "5cf6f434-9fed-5970-b8e2-d17962421f47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e02cd42-42d0-5705-a005-75524f936d99" :answer/id #uuid "6e02cd42-42d0-5705-a005-75524f936d99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afae2ee4-4ec6-5251-afbc-9e17da7a7304" :answer/id #uuid "afae2ee4-4ec6-5251-afbc-9e17da7a7304" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab558918-6e8c-59eb-9a7e-4397683388cc" :answer/id #uuid "ab558918-6e8c-59eb-9a7e-4397683388cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-411917dc-6c93-55d0-978d-0767736c6121" :answer/id #uuid "411