answer.type/text :answer/value "increasing" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97b00b3e-da29-54ff-83d5-338841b50f95" :answer/id #uuid "97b00b3e-da29-54ff-83d5-338841b50f95" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "integrable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab09ceb5-4998-5c22-be12-7d14477b6cf3" :answer/id #uuid "ab09ceb5-4998-5c22-be12-7d14477b6cf3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "surjective" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c17b3d7-202b-5319-afd9-fd4e982a5f27" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c17b3d7-202b-5319-afd9-fd4e982a5f27" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-a78cd018-7cd8-5d14-9406-9f833da673ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a78cd018-7cd8-5d14-9406-9f833da673ca" "answer-c52d2593-5aa0-50cc-b823-33d26cf2df16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a78cd018-7cd8-5d14-9406-9f833da673ca" :answer/id #uuid "a78cd018-7cd8-5d14-9406-9f833da673ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|A|=|B|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c52d2593-5aa0-50cc-b823-33d26cf2df16" :answer/id #uuid "c52d2593-5aa0-50cc-b823-33d26cf2df16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|A|\\ne |B|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2a225633-5b65-55d6-85ca-1c31e57a48fb" :question/id #uuid "2a225633-5b65-55d6-85ca-1c31e57a48fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-216534" :question/problem "$$\nA=N,\\,B={3,1,-1,-3,…}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-2 \\\\  & =1-3 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${b}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =3+(n-1)(-2) \\\\  & =3-2n+2 \\\\  & =5-2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB={5-2n∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $⋯$ | $5-2n$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=5-2n\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ca0f8227-1a0c-5fab-8039-c300e8dd0d1b" "answer-field-251d7581-c688-5919-9c12-44035ef581ef" "answer-field-c48e800f-2bae-5217-9921-20bb0d6952bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ca0f8227-1a0c-5fab-8039-c300e8dd0d1b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca0f8227-1a0c-5fab-8039-c300e8dd0d1b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8cfcd3d8-9919-53d3-8597-0ccd5d2bca68" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35e6a49c-a4eb-523c-873d-ff7261d25779" "answer-b64bcccb-29d6-519a-944f-e0fc1d42d047" "answer-8cfcd3d8-9919-53d3-8597-0ccd5d2bca68" "answer-31c4e142-4b45-5e7c-8fdb-43c57763a277" "answer-1ae3b426-2b52-5c1a-9ed9-6dc93a8c8fa7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35e6a49c-a4eb-523c-873d-ff7261d25779" :answer/id #uuid "35e6a49c-a4eb-523c-873d-ff7261d25779" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2n+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b64bcccb-29d6-519a-944f-e0fc1d42d047" :answer/id #uuid "b64bcccb-29d6-519a-944f-e0fc1d42d047" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3n-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cfcd3d8-9919-53d3-8597-0