wer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d963030-e5ea-5806-93b1-1db562964c9e" :answer/id #uuid "5d963030-e5ea-5806-93b1-1db562964c9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|A|=|B|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1180da69-070a-5252-b980-f5c18aa9cc6c" :answer/id #uuid "1180da69-070a-5252-b980-f5c18aa9cc6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|A|\\ne |B|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b75659ad-fe25-5e83-878e-b9945eaf6be7" :question/id #uuid "b75659ad-fe25-5e83-878e-b9945eaf6be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-181659" :question/problem "$$\nA=N,\\,B={-1,1,3,5,…}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =2 \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${b}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-1+(n-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1+2n-2 \\\\  & =2n-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB={2n-3∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $⋯$ | $2n-3$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=2n-3\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc890179-dc7f-5f57-91