of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-2 \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2+(n-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2-2n+2 \\\\  & =-2n+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA={-2n+4∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $⋯$ | $-2n+4$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=-2n+4\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff14ff76-eafd-553a-b96d-1e02876d3683" "answer-field-1fdcb9f4-a089-5967-bef1-65cdb34a1067" "answer-field-647967d8-50ff-580a-be1b-552992bcb78c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff14ff76-eafd-553a-b96d-1e02876d3683" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff14ff76-eafd-553a-b96d-1e02876d3683" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-96de4f09-9c29-5916-bca8-81e8413b1e93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96de4f09-9c29-5916-bca8-81e8413b1e93" "answer-e91c11d5-ad90-5a02-837f-30cf665abf94" "answer-7e0f7338-ebdf-5402-9f3b-e915edca15ac" "answer-144f1a7e-f5ee-5b2b-9875-8c4c251dd99a" "answer-cdaf4c08-1b80-54b9-9e35-f6320fa2282f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96de4f09-9c29-5916-bca8-81e8413b1e93" :answer/id #uuid "96de4f09-9c29-5916-bca8-81e8413b1e93" :answer/type