 #uuid "647967d8-50ff-580a-be1b-552992bcb78c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-80ec5d10-6914-5422-a33f-2bbb9d82efd0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80ec5d10-6914-5422-a33f-2bbb9d82efd0" "answer-ddcfdd85-1202-523d-8ea5-972757b85095"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80ec5d10-6914-5422-a33f-2bbb9d82efd0" :answer/id #uuid "80ec5d10-6914-5422-a33f-2bbb9d82efd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|A|=|B|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddcfdd85-1202-523d-8ea5-972757b85095" :answer/id #uuid "ddcfdd85-1202-523d-8ea5-972757b85095" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|A|\\ne |B|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ec2d5fbd-6cc2-569f-94cc-7ef69e7f43a0" :question/id #uuid "ec2d5fbd-6cc2-569f-94cc-7ef69e7f43a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-181749" :question/problem "$$\nA=N,\\,B={2,6,18,54,…}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB={2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $2$ | $6$ | $18$ | $54$ | $⋯$ | $2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0e1e02f5-f6c5-5aa0-ba17-d5174f5aace6" "answer-field-ee38ff5a-4d45-5456-b2ba-19cd9120e6cb" "answer-field-ad5463f6-c723-5d56-b542-fcfdfcd31f61"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0e1e02f5-f6c5-5aa0-ba17-d5174f5aace6" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e1e02f5-f6c5-5aa0-ba17-d5174f5aace6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-eeeea20b-0914-5fb2-84c3-ad27f37632ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eeeea20b-0914-5fb2-84c3-ad27f37632ff" "answer-782f6fc0-88b4-5ed1-8e6b-b224a9823a35" "answer-705db018-fef4-583d-8f8c-91419b7e2819" "answer-9069b9f1-9d07-532b-a780-732975a00eca" "answer-6c029dc4-9cdd-5258-b330-6f2a04deee63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeeea20b-0914-5fb2-84c3-ad27f37632ff" :answer/id #uuid "eeeea20b-0914-5fb2-84c3-ad27f37632ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-782f6fc0-88b4-5ed1-8e6b-b224a9823a35" :answer/id #uuid "782f6fc0-88b4-5ed1-8e6b-b224a9823a35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2n-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-705db018-fef4-583d-8f8c-91419b7e2819" :answer/id #uuid "705db018-fef4-583d-8f8c-91419b7e2819" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9069b9f1-9d07-532b-a780-732975a00eca" :answer/id #uuid "9069b9f1-9d07-532b-a780-732975a00eca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{n-1}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c029dc4-9cdd-5258-b330-6f2a04deee63" :answer/id #uuid "6c029dc4-9cdd-5258-b330-6f2a04deee63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answe