4f15-f70f-5bf2-aa53-6e682a54a3eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-181750" :question/problem "$$\nA=N,\\,B={2,10,50,250,…}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB={2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $2$ | $10$ | $50$ | $250$ | $⋯$ | $2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c0e36464-4d7c-58e2-9665-8ca7c7e8a510" "answer-field-dc713b9e-3143-5922-9790-954bdf150692" "answer-field-5b7ae9b9-5aac-5ea8-b929-98bbeac7e2a1" "answer-field-9fabcfc6-35e5-5223-a078-41b9cc1a6815"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c0e36464-4d7c-58e2-9665-8ca7c7e8a510" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e36464-4d7c-58e2-9665-8ca7c7e8a510" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-d096328e-4a5b-5e5f-9090-a973c13