value "|A|=|B|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eee3a5e6-fd55-599e-89da-080807eb602c" :answer/id #uuid "eee3a5e6-fd55-599e-89da-080807eb602c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|A|\\ne |B|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a1686501-a304-5a7a-bf6f-4c7790651929" :question/id #uuid "a1686501-a304-5a7a-bf6f-4c7790651929" :question/math-academy-id "q-216550" :question/problem "$$\nA=N,\\,B={18,\\,12,\\,8,\\,\\frac{16}{3},\\,…}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB={18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\,|\\,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $18$ | $12$ | $8$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $⋯$ | $18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8924976-ac1d-5805-ab82-ea80f8fb3b09" "answer-field-8285bc18-0c23-50a